The ten rare rows are the reason the bar is every seed and not 'seen once'. Their reason texts go
with the bins when the manifests are re-rendered; the correction is recorded here because the
file that carried the wrong word will not exist to carry the correction.

ONE BIN IS NOT MEASURED HERE, named in the checker's UNMEASURABLE table so that an unmapped bin
fails the run instead of passing quietly:
  gen_cmp_imm_edges_cg.cp_cj_off.self
My first reading of it was WRONG and is corrected here rather than quietly dropped. I argued that
a compressed jump with a zero offset branches to itself and is therefore exclusive with program
termination. The committed round-1 merged report refutes that: gen_round_0/gen_grpinfo.txt, User
Defined Bins for cp_cj_off, reads self 398 beside pos_rand 308 and neg_rand 114, five bins
expected and none uncovered. A jump to itself is the park-here idiom a test ends with, and the
testbench ends the run, so the program never has to leave the loop. What my measurement supports
is ENTRY-SCOPED and nothing more: this entry's programs emit compressed jumps, none with a zero
offset, the non-zero offsets being the control. The bin is credited from the merge.

THE ALIGNMENT READING COUNTS ONLY WHAT IT CAN PROVE. cr_insn_align bins are properties of the
assembled address, so the checker walks the generator's own line_size model from each .balign 4
anchor. That walk is exact only while every line since the anchor has a size the assembler cannot
change: a plain mnemonic inside .option rvc may be compressed, and the walk cannot know. The
reader therefore stops placing instances after the first such line and reports the shortfall as
UNPROVEN rather than as a failure. The GREEN run carries UNPROVEN rows for the memory forms for
exactly this reason, and they are NOT a claim that those bins are short. The simulation settles
them: in the companion log both entries hit every declared bin, alignment bins included, at all
forty seeds.

